    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d572">

                <pb facs="0845" n="825" sameAs="frus:frus1950v07/pg825"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795B.11/9–2950</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. Frank
                            Ragusa of the Division of Security Affairs to the Assistant
                        Secretary of State for Far Eastern Affairs</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Rusk</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1950-09-29T15:30:00-05:00">September 29, 1950—3:30 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Dulles had Miss Doyle dictate the following message to you by phone:</p>
                <p>I hope we shall not give the impression of being disposed now with victory to
                    ditch Syngman Rhee. He is far from perfect but he is a great patriot and
                    anti-communist and organized his country so that they put up a good fight, and
                    he is not as bad as Communist smear portrays him. If the Chiang Kai-shek episode
                    is followed by what seems a similar treatment of Rhee, then it will seem to
                    establish a pattern which would, I think, greatly discourage any Asiatic leaders
                    from wanting to fight on our side.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d572-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank Ragusa</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d572-f1" n="1" xml:id="d572-f1-content">
                <p>The source text bore the following two manuscript notations: “I agree. D[ean]
                    R[usk]” and “So do I. U A[lexis] J[ohnson]”.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
